  "summary": "Traditional Finnish settlements organized around churches with distinctive village layouts.",
  "description": "Finnish church villages feature clustered buildings arranged around a central church, often with a common green space and connecting pathways. This layout developed due to Finland's sparse population and the church's central role in community life, creating self-sufficient agricultural communities.",
  "culturalContext": "Finnish church villages (kirkonkylä) — UNESCO-listed Petäjävesi Old Church, Sonkajärvi, the Sastamala clusters — organized rural Finland around timber churches: not-gated freeholds (erämaaluvat) where farmers built cabins for feast-weekends, bell towers as village gateposts, and wooden vernacular (log-and-craft) united by church roads. The pattern — church, bell tower, rectory, stable-cabins — structures Finland's rural landscape and Protestant-vernacular identity; Petäjävesi's 1765 log church is the movement's masterpiece.",
